🟡 Topic 2: Core Commands & State (VERY IMPORTANT ⚠️)

This is frequently asked in interviews + critical in real projects

🔹 Core Terraform Commands
1. terraform init

👉 Initializes your project

Downloads providers (AWS, etc.)
Prepares working directory
terraform init
2. terraform plan

👉 Shows what Terraform will do

terraform plan

✔ Example output:

+ create → new resource
~ update → modify
- destroy → delete

👉 Safe command (no changes applied)

3. terraform apply

👉 Actually creates/updates infra

terraform apply
Executes plan
Prompts for approval (yes)
4. terraform destroy

👉 Deletes all resources

terraform destroy

⚠️ Important in cost control (AWS billing!)

🔹 Terraform State (🔥 CRITICAL CONCEPT)
What is State?
Stored in: terraform.tfstate
Keeps track of:
Resources created
Their current status
Mapping between config & real infra

👉 Without state → Terraform doesn’t know what exists

🔹 Example

You create EC2 → state file stores:

{
  "resources": [
    "aws_instance.demo"
  ]
}
🔹 Why State is Important?
Tracks infrastructure
Detects changes (drift)
Prevents duplication
🔴 Problem: Local State (Not Good for Teams)

If stored locally:

❌ Not shared
❌ Risk of overwrite
❌ No locking
🔹 Solution: Remote State

Use:

AWS S3 → store state
DynamoDB → locking

👉 Setup example:

terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket         = "my-terraform-state"
    key            = "dev/terraform.tfstate"
    region         = "ap-south-1"
    dynamodb_table = "terraform-lock"
  }
}
🔹 State Locking (VERY IMPORTANT 🔥)

Problem:

2 engineers run apply → conflict 💥

Solution:

DynamoDB lock ensures:
Only one execution at a time
🔹 Drift Detection

👉 Drift = Manual change in AWS outside Terraform

Example:

You change EC2 type in AWS console

👉 terraform plan will:

Detect mismatch
Try to fix it
